A handler written in a runtime instance takes the callback's host signature.

On the Model a node's handler is promoted to a method, and emitIRPromotedHandler
gives that method the Go signature of the callback it is attached to: fyne's
`OnChanged` is `func(bool)` on a check and `func(string)` on an entry, and a
gtk4 signal trampoline passes nothing. An instance record's handlers are
lambdas assigned where they are written, and reached the host as SNGL wrote
them -- `func()` for a checkbox, a payload struct for an input -- so a record
holding either did not build. A checkbox with no `@change` was the same
failure through the empty handler the override hands it, which is what
`sngl:ui/markup`'s task list item is.

The lambda takes the override's own parameters, and its body reads them as
the locals they are. `Row` declares a `var on` and the checkbox override names
its parameter `on`: read by name, the write-back assigned the row's state to
itself (`c.checked__inst0 = c.on`).

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `OnChanged = func\(\)` -- a check's callback takes the bool
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `= c\.on\n\t\tc\.__n` -- the write-back reads the callback's parameter, not the row's var of the same name
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:test"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

component Row(label string) node {
    var on = false
    var typed = ""

    hbox {
        checkbox(checked=on, @change { on = !on })
        checkbox(checked=true, disabled=true)
        input(value=typed, @input(e) { typed = e })
        text(value="{label} {on} {typed}")
    }
}

component main node {
    var items = ["a", "b"]

    vbox {
        for var it = items {
            Row(label=it)
        }
    }
}

func testRows(t Test, c main) {
    t.assert(c.items.length == 2)
}

window {
    main
}
